{#
  T-6.01 — the order card. One component, every variant.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.orders.card' with { order: order } only %}

  Parameters:
    order  required — one item of the `orders` collection.

  «ALL THREE SUPPLIED VARIANTS COVERED BY ONE COMPONENT» IS WHAT THIS FILE IS
  FOR, AND THE VARIANTS TURN OUT NOT TO BE VARIANTS. `Orders In Progress.pdf`,
  `Orders Pending Payment.pdf` and `Full Page.pdf` draw the same card three
  times; only the status pill and the two buttons at the foot differ, and both of
  those are already conditional on flags the order carries. There is no `variant`
  parameter here because there is nothing for it to switch.

  THE ACTIONS ARE THE PLATFORM'S GATES, NOT A GUESS AT WHICH GROUP THE ORDER IS
  IN. `can_cancel`, `can_reorder`, `can_rate` and `is_pending_payment` are the
  order's own booleans, and every button is behind the one that authorises it —
  the same standard T-5.15 met by letting `salla-user-menu` decide whether a
  store has loyalty. A cancelled order therefore shows no «تقييم الطلب» even
  though `Full Page.pdf` draws one on its cancelled card, because the platform
  says that order cannot be rated and the artboard is illustrative about which
  order it drew, not about the rule.

  «تحميل الفاتورة» AND «تتبع الطلب» COME OUT OF `order.links`, LABEL INCLUDED.
  That array is documented as `{url, label, type}` with type in
  ['print','shipment','digital'] — the platform's own link and the platform's own
  wording. Writing either string into the theme would have been a second name for
  something Salla already names, and it would appear on orders that have no
  invoice and no shipment to point at.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN IMPROVISED. The artboards draw
  a shipping address under «عنوان الشحن» — «الرياض – السعودية - النخيل …». **No
  documented field on the order carries it.** `order.shipping` is the shipping
  *company*; `order.packages[]` is shipments; the customer's addresses are a
  separate API (`salla.api.request('address')`) with no link back to an order.
  Inventing a field name would fail silently on a live store, so the row keeps
  the half that is sourced — the heading and the status pill — and the line
  itself is raised as **OP-8**.

  THE COLLECTION'S DEPTH IS GUARDED, FOR A REASON THAT IS NOT PARANOIA. Upstream's
  own pre-component Twig list (commit 6aca3321^) proves `reference_id`, `url`,
  `total`, `created_at`, `status`, `is_rated` and `source` are on the collection.
  It uses nothing else, so nothing else is proven, and `salla-orders`' API route
  trims `items[]` to `{name, quantity}` — evidence that list payloads *can* be
  thinner than the single-order model. Every block past the proven set is behind
  an `{% if %}`: on a full collection the card is the artboard, and on a trimmed
  one it is shorter rather than broken. See OP-9.
#}
{% set order_id = order.id ?: order.reference_id %}

<article class="order-card account-panel"
         data-order-card
         data-order-pending-payment="{{ order.is_pending_payment ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
         data-order-cancellable="{{ order.can_cancel ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
         aria-labelledby="order-heading-{{ order_id }}">

    <div class="order-card__header">
        <div class="order-card__identity">
            {#
              The number is the way to the order's own page. Upstream put a bare
              `<a>` over every table cell; one named link is the same route with
              an accessible name on it.
            #}
            <h3 class="order-card__id" id="order-heading-{{ order_id }}">
                <a href="{{ order.url }}">
                    <span class="sr-only">{{ trans('pages.thank_you.order_id') }}</span>
                    #{{ order.reference_id }}
                </a>
            </h3>

            {#
              `datetime` is the machine-readable half. The visible format is the
              artboard's 20/09/2025; the attribute is what a parser reads, and
              the two never disagree because both come off the same value.
            #}
            <time class="order-card__date" datetime="{{ order.created_at|date('Y-m-d') }}">
                {{ order.created_at|date('d/m/Y') }}
            </time>
        </div>

        <div class="order-card__summary">
            <span class="order-card__total">
                <span class="sr-only">{{ trans('pages.orders.total') }}</span>
                {{ order.total|money }}
            </span>

            {#
              HIDDEN UNTIL THE SCRIPT ARRIVES, WHICH IS THE POINT. A collapse
              control with no script behind it is a button that lies. The card
              renders expanded — the state all three artboards draw — and
              `order-list.js` removes `hidden` once it can actually honour a
              press. Where JS never runs, the card is simply a card.
            #}
            <button type="button"
                    class="order-card__toggle"
                    aria-expanded="true"
                    aria-controls="order-body-{{ order_id }}"
                    data-order-toggle
                    hidden>
                <span class="order-card__marker" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="sr-only">{{ trans('theme.orders.toggle_details', { ':number': order.reference_id }) }}</span>
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="order-card__body" id="order-body-{{ order_id }}">
        {% if order.items|length %}
            <ul class="order-card__items">
                {% for item in order.items %}
                    {#
                      The row is `components.orders.item` — the same partial
                      `orders/single.twig` renders, so the list and the order's
                      own page cannot drift into two different product rows.
                    #}
                    {% include 'components.orders.item' with { item: item } only %}
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        {% endif %}

        <div class="order-card__shipping">
            <h4 class="order-card__shipping-title">{{ trans('theme.orders.shipping_address') }}</h4>
            {% include 'components.orders.status' with { status: order.status } only %}
        </div>

        {#
          THE TOTALS ARE SALLA'S COMPONENT, NOT FOUR ROWS RE-DERIVED HERE.
          `salla-order-totals-card` renders exactly what the artboard draws —
          «مجموع المنتجات», the discount lines with the coupon's own name and
          percent, «تكلفة التوصيل», «الإجمالي» — off the order object, with the
          platform's strings. "No client-side arithmetic" is met by there being no
          arithmetic anywhere: the theme hands over the order and prints what
          comes back. Its own «ملخص الطلب» heading is not in the artboard and is
          hidden in the stylesheet.
        #}
        <salla-order-totals-card class="order-card__totals" order="{{ order }}"></salla-order-totals-card>

        {% for link in order.links|default([]) if link.type == 'print' %}
            <a class="order-card__invoice" href="{{ link.url }}">
                {#
                  `printer2` is upstream's own glyph for this link on the order
                  page. Picking a nicer-sounding name risks a glyph the icon font
                  does not carry, which renders as an empty box.
                #}
                {% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'printer2', size: 'md' } only %}
                <span>{{ link.label }}</span>
            </a>
        {% endfor %}

        <div class="order-card__actions">
            {% if order.is_pending_payment %}
                {#
                  Upstream's own reading of an expired window: `pending_payment_ends_in == 0`
                  means the link no longer leads anywhere, so it is a sentence
                  rather than a button that fails on arrival.
                #}
                {% if order.pending_payment_ends_in == 0 %}
                    <p class="order-card__expired">{{ trans('pages.orders.pending_payment_expired') }}</p>
                {% else %}
                    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                        label:   trans('pages.orders.finish_payment'),
                        href:    order.payment_url,
                        variant: 'primary',
                        class:   'order-card__action'
                    } only %}
                {% endif %}
            {% else %}
                {% for link in order.links|default([]) if link.type == 'shipment' %}
                    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                        label:   link.label,
                        href:    link.url,
                        variant: 'primary',
                        class:   'order-card__action'
                    } only %}
                {% endfor %}

                {% if order.can_reorder %}
                    {#
                      The hook is a selector and nothing else; T-6.04 binds it.
                      Reordering is an API call with a confirmation in front of
                      it, and putting that behaviour in a card partial would put
                      it on every page that ever renders a card.
                    #}
                    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                        label:   trans('pages.orders.reorder'),
                        variant: 'primary',
                        class:   'order-card__action',
                        attrs:   { 'data-order-reorder': order_id }
                    } only %}
                {% endif %}

                {% if order.can_rate %}
                    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                        label:   trans('pages.rating.rate'),
                        href:    order.url,
                        variant: 'secondary',
                        class:   'order-card__action'
                    } only %}
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}

            {% if order.can_cancel %}
                {#
                  T-6.03: `data-sheet-open` names the page's single cancel dialog
                  and `data-order-cancel` says which order it is about. One
                  dialog serves the whole list — see `cancel-dialog.twig`.
                #}
                {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
                    label:   trans('pages.orders.cancel'),
                    variant: 'error',
                    class:   'order-card__action order-card__action--cancel',
                    attrs:   { 'data-order-cancel': order_id, 'data-sheet-open': 'cancel-order-dialog' }
                } only %}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
